      <h2 id="buying-guide">Buying Guide: How to Choose a Password Manager in 2026</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Step 1 — Start free:</strong> Bitwarden Free is the best starting point. If you need more polish, try 1Password's 14-day trial. Do not pay on day one.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 2 — Check security architecture:</strong> Look for zero-knowledge encryption, open-source code, and third-party audits. 1Password's Secret Key and Bitwarden's open-source codebase are the strongest security signals.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 3 — Evaluate autofill:</strong> The best password manager is the one that autofills reliably across all your devices. Test on your most-used login forms.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 4 — Check cross-platform:</strong> Verify browser extensions for Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge, plus mobile apps for iOS and Android.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 5 — Consider family or team plans:</strong> Family plans from 1Password ($4.99/5 people) and Bitwarden ($40/year/family) are excellent value. Business plans depend on compliance needs.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Learn more about our <a href="/how-we-test/">testing methodology</a> and <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the best password manager in 2026?</summary>
          <p>1Password is the best overall password manager in 2026 because it combines zero-knowledge encryption, the most polished cross-platform apps, Travel Mode for border security, and a family plan that covers 5 people for $4.99/month. Bitwarden is the best free option, with unlimited passwords on unlimited devices at $0.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is Bitwarden really free?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Bitwarden's personal free tier includes unlimited passwords on unlimited devices, cross-platform sync, browser extensions, and basic two-factor authentication. There is no time limit, no credit card required, and no nag screens. The $10/year premium tier adds 2FA token storage, encrypted file attachments, and priority support, but the free tier is fully usable indefinitely.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is 1Password worth paying for?</summary>
          <p>1Password is worth paying for if you value the most polished autofill experience, Travel Mode for international travel, Watchtower breach alerts, and a family plan for shared vaults. At $2.99/month for individuals and $4.99/month for families of 5, it is affordable. If you only need basic password storage and want free, Bitwarden covers the same core functionality at $0.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which password manager is best for business teams?</summary>
          <p>Keeper is the best password manager for enterprise and compliance-driven teams because it offers SOC 2, FedRAMP, HIPAA, and PCI compliance certifications, role-based access control, audit logging, and delegated administration. 1Password Teams is better for small teams that want simplicity. Bitwarden Teams is the most affordable business option starting at $3/user/month.</p>
        </details>
